fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1074", description: some(value: "scaland twin of WI-995 (rust side DELIVERED, commit b16e44b8): an import is spent in the file that lists it — and the rule is keyed on the SYMBOL, not the name.\n\nTHE RULE (user decision 2026-08-04, delivered rust-side): an import resolves ONLY in the\nfile whose text lists it, and it is SPENT at name resolution — nothing after the symbol\ntable reads it. Scaland reproduces the pre-rule divergence exactly, via symbol identity\ninstead of TermId hash-consing:\n  - a scope IS the address: `opaque type ScopeId = TermSymbol` (intern/SymbolTable.scala:75),\n    and `define` merges by short name (:184), so `namespace demo` opened in two files —\n    or `sort X` next to `namespace X` — is ONE scope entry;\n  - one shared import table per address: `Scope.imports: HashMap[String, TermSymbol]` (:89),\n    written by `addImport` (:201) from pass-2 `processImports` (load/Loader.scala);\n  - sole reader: `resolveRecursive` step 1b (:227) — the line-for-line mirror of rust's\n    `resolve_in_scope_recursive` step 1b. So WI-995 fixtures B and D (an import written in a\n    separate file at the same address changes a bare name in an unedited file) reproduce here.\n\nWHAT THE RUST DELIVERY SETTLED — inherit, do not re-decide:\n  - corpus cost MEASURED ZERO (counterfactual double-answer audit, six groups, 10.9k-24.1k\n    resolutions; 238/250 imports selective, 0 wildcard, 0 top-level), so the rule lands\n    directly — no warning/migration phase;\n  - THE RULE IS KEYED ON THE SYMBOL, not the name: `imports` is last-write-wins, so asking\n    \"did some visible file write this NAME\" still lets file A read file B's target by load\n    order. The check must ask \"did THIS file's import bind THIS symbol\". Fixture D cannot\n    catch this (its foreign import is the only writer of the name); the TWO-WRITER fixture\n    (two files import the same short name from different targets at one address) is the\n    control and must fail with the fix backed out;\n  - an import writes BOTH an alias (`addImport`) and, for a wildcard, a parent link\n    (`addParent(scope, p, isEnclosing = false)`, WI-988) — both must be file-scoped, and\n    they are suppressed by different predicates.\n\nSCALAND-SPECIFIC SHAPE. There is no source registry — `Span.file: String`\n(span/Span.scala:53) is the existing file-identity carrier, and the loader is already\nper-file (`scanDefinitions(kb, files)` / `loadAll`). Thread a file identity (rust:\nSourceId + ImportOrigin) through addImport/addParent and into resolveInScope so step 1b\nhonors only the resolving file's own imports. Rust's `ImportOrigin::Invocation`\n(`anthill query -i`) has no scaland analog today — state the gap at the seam rather than\ninventing one. Prelude/global registrations (load/Prelude.scala:182 wires global imports\nfor reflect entities) get the treatment rust gave `_global`: a synthetic origin, exempt by\nconstruction, stated at its site. Expect suite migrations where a test relied on an import\nre-exporting through the address (rust had 12); each is a fix to the test's imports, not a\ncounterexample to the rule.\n\nACCEPTANCE: WI-995 fixtures A-D ported to scaland (B and D flip to the file-local answer,\nA and C unchanged) plus the two-writer symbol-keying fixture, each DRIVING resolution\n(resolve the bare name, assert WHICH symbol it binds), with the two-writer test verified to\nfail with the fix backed out; sbt test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-11T08:23:48Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1074", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. The rule, mirrored from rust b16e44b8: every import write carries its ImportOrigin (Builtin/File/Declaration); Scope.imports keeps EVERY write with its writer (symbol-keyed, last-VISIBLE-write wins, in-file recency preserved); a parent link carries its origin ON ScopeInclusion (diverges from rust's side table deliberately - scaland's parents list is per-write append-only, so the justification rides the link and an edge two producers justify is two links). File identity = FileId minted per ParsedFile INSTANCE (SymbolTable.fileIdOf, identity map; a name string would alias every default '<input>' parse); asking-file cursor set per file in all four scan passes + load, cleared after scan, LEFT after load (the load-then-resolve shape; post-loadAll cursor = LAST file, documented as an order artifact). Prelude reflect aliases = Builtin (the _global ruling). No Invocation case - scaland has no query invocation; gap stated at the enum. PendingImport/processImports typed ImportOrigin.File so the pass-4 retry has no dead arm. LANDMINES: (1) pass-3 mint + shared address: a foreign file's 'rule f' mints an ADDRESS-SCOPED local that shadows your import - RUST PARITY (verified via anthill query, answer 3 both orders), pinned by the mint-parity test, which passes with fix and without it because a deferred (WI-295) alias does not exist yet when the mint guard asks; (2) addImport must MOVE a repeated (origin,sym) write to last, not drop it, or import a/b/a answers b. Back-out verified: B, D, D-wildcard, two-writer, pass-2-seam fail with originVisible forced true; A, C, mint-parity pass by design. Suite migrations: ZERO (451 green untouched), matching rust's zero corpus cost. /code-review high run; findings fixed inline (recency, ImportOrigin.File narrowing, ScopeInclusion origin, LoadFixture single-homing, retention docs).", at: "2026-08-11T08:23:52Z")
